---
import { SitePage } from '@boombapbot/editor';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Base
  title="About · boombapbot"
  description="boombapbot is a drum machine that runs in your browser: what it is, how it is made, and what it keeps."
>
  <SitePage client:load baseUrl={base} current="about">
    <h1>About boombapbot</h1>
    <p class="sb-lede">
      boombapbot is a drum machine that runs in your browser. There is nothing to install, you don't need an
      account, and what you play stays on your computer.
    </p>

    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>
      The page is one machine, the 808: sixteen sounds, sixteen step buttons, and the knobs and switches that
      shape them. It works the way the original's operation manual describes. You pick an instrument, press the
      steps it should play, and the machine remembers thirty-two patterns that you can play by hand or string
      into a track.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <a href={`${base}docs`}>docs</a> go through every control. Or just open <a href={base}>the machine</a>
      and press start.
    </p>

    <h2>How it is made</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The sounds are synthesised.</strong> Each one is built from its knob settings as you turn them,
        the way the circuits did it. Nothing is a recording. I tuned it by ear and haven't compared it with a
        real machine, so treat it as a tribute, not a copy.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>One engine keeps the time.</strong> The sequencer and the sounds are a single program that runs
        on the browser's audio thread. Every step is placed on the audio clock, so the beat holds steady whatever
        else the page is doing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The faceplate is drawn.</strong> It is original artwork, generated from the same layout the
        controls use. It is not a photograph.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What it keeps, and where</h2>
    <p>
      Your rhythms, tracks and knob positions are saved in this browser every time you change something, and
      they are there when you come back. They are not sent anywhere unless you save a beat to an account. To keep
      a copy or move to another device, export the project file from settings, or save the beat to a free account
      and load it there; you can also export audio and MIDI.
    </p>
    <p>
      The site loads its own files and nothing else. There are no analytics and no ads. Making an account is
      optional, and logging in sets the site's one cookie.
    </p>

    <h2>What is not here yet</h2>
    <p>
      I haven't built the other three machines in the header yet. Same for upgrades and the shop, which is why
      those two buttons in the footer only say so for now.
    </p>

    <h2>Who made it</h2>
    <p>
      One person. I have a master's in software engineering, I work in QA, I'm autistic, and I love writing code
      about as much as I love electronic music. boombapbot is what happens when those overlap for long enough. I
      build it in my spare time, on a little too much coffee and a little too much stress.
    </p>
    <p>
      It's an independent project, and it isn't affiliated with or endorsed by the makers of the machines it's
      modelled on. The <a href={`${base}policies`}>policies</a> page has the rest. If something's broken, or you
      just want to say hi, I'm at
      <a href="https://mm.coffee" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">mm.coffee</a>.
    </p>
  </SitePage>
</Base>
